Given the following four propositions:
(1) If two lines in a plane are parallel to another plane, then these two planes are parallel to each other;
(2) Two lines that are perpendicular to the same line are parallel to each other;
(3) Through a point outside a plane, there is exactly one line parallel to the given plane;
(4) Two lines that are perpendicular to the same plane are parallel.

Among these, the number of true propositions is ( )
A: 0
B: 1
C: 2
D: 3

s:
(1) Since two intersecting lines in a plane that are both parallel to another plane imply that these two planes are parallel to each other, and the word "intersecting" is not mentioned in (1),
∴ (1) is incorrect;
(2) In space, two lines perpendicular to the same line may be skew, intersecting, or parallel, hence (2) is incorrect;
(3) Through a point outside a plane, there are countless lines parallel to the given plane, thus (3) is incorrect;
(4) Two lines that are perpendicular to the same plane are parallel, which is correct.
Therefore, the correct choice is $\boxed{\text{B}}$.

