s:
Since when "a=1", the statement "the function $f(x) = |x-1|$ is increasing on the interval $[1, +\infty)$" is true,  
it implies that "a=1" $\Rightarrow$ "the function $f(x) = |x-a|$ is increasing on the interval $[1, +\infty)$" is a true proposition;  
Since when "the function $f(x) = |x-a|$ is increasing on the interval $[1, +\infty)$", we have $a \leq 1$,  
it implies that "the function $f(x) = |x-a|$ is increasing on the interval $[1, +\infty)$" $\Rightarrow$ "a=1" is a false proposition;  
Therefore, "a=1" is a sufficient but not necessary condition for the function $f(x) = |x-a|$ to be increasing on the interval $[1, +\infty)$;  
Hence, the answer is $\boxed{\text{sufficient but not necessary}}$.

